---
// src/components/OtherIslandSection.astro
//
// Closing full-bleed illustration for the bottom of the page — not
// part of the flanking-islands family (ArtIslandSection /
// ComputerIslandSection / EducationIslandSection /
// AchievementIslandSection). No text, no mouse-repel, no bob loop —
// just one image that always spans the full viewport width, edge to
// edge, regardless of screen size.
//
// "Full viewport width no matter what" is done with the standard
// break-out-of-the-container trick:
//   width: 100vw; position: relative; left: 50%; right: 50%;
//   margin-left: -50vw; margin-right: -50vw;
// `width: 100%` alone would only match the width of whatever contains
// it. Right now nothing in Layout.astro constrains that (it's a bare
// `<slot />` in `<body>`), so `100%` would likely look identical today
// — but the 100vw break-out doesn't depend on that staying true, so
// this keeps working even if a max-width wrapper gets added around
// the page later. `overflow-x: hidden` on the section guards against
// the one common side effect of this trick: 100vw includes the
// vertical scrollbar's width, which can introduce a few px of
// horizontal overflow on some browsers.
//
// Height on wide screens is unconstrained (`height: auto`), so the
// image keeps its own aspect ratio and the whole illustration shows —
// your current source is a wide/landscape image, so this looks right
// at desktop widths without any cropping.
//
// On narrow screens that same wide aspect ratio flattens out into a
// short strip (a ~16:9-ish image at 375px wide is only ~210px tall),
// which read as "barely covers a third of the screen" — not enough
// presence for a closing illustration.
//
// Fixed the same way BonusIslandSection's background scales: under
// `max-width: 640px`, the section gets a real viewport-height box, and
// the image switches to `object-fit: cover` so it fills that box by
// cropping instead of flattening out.
//
// That box has to be `height: 100vh`, not `min-height: 100vh` — this
// tripped up the first attempt at this fix. `.other-section-image`
// fills it via `height: 100%`, and a percentage height on a child only
// resolves against a parent that has a DEFINITE height; a `min-height`
// alone doesn't count as definite (per spec, it's a constraint, not a
// used value), so the image's `100%` was computing back to `auto` —
// meaning the image rendered at its natural (short) aspect-ratio
// height same as before, while the section around it was genuinely
// 100vh tall, and the gap between the two showed up as blank white
// space at the bottom. `height: 100vh` gives the child something
// definite to resolve against, so `height: 100%` on the image actually
// means something. Safe to use a hard `height` here (rather than the
// usual "prefer min-height" advice) because this section's only
// content on mobile is the image itself — there's nothing else that
// could need more room and get clipped.
//
// `object-position: top` (rather than `center`) is deliberate — this
// image's TOP edge is the one that has to match BonusIslandSection's
// bottom (see the shared `.sky-chain` seam-matching in Layout.astro),
// so keeping that edge protected from cropping matters; whatever gets
// trimmed off is the bottom of the illustration instead, which doesn't
// need to line up with anything since this is the last section on the
// page.
//
// No seam-fade classes any more — the shared fade-band system that
// used to blend section edges together was removed per your request,
// so this section now just butts directly against BonusIslandSection.
//
// Now also carries `.halftone-hover`, same as the rest of the chain —
// joins the single, page-wide cursor-spotlight element in
// Layout.astro (`.halftone-spotlight`) purely by having this class,
// which is all `body:has(.halftone-hover:hover) .halftone-spotlight`
// needs to turn the spotlight on while the cursor's over this
// section. No per-section markup or script needed for it any more —
// an earlier version added a `.halftone-overlay` div + script to
// every section individually, but that ran into exactly this
// section's own `overflow-x: hidden` (needed for the 100vw break-out
// above) computing its `overflow-y` to `auto` per the CSS overflow
// spec, which clipped that per-section overlay's bleed and cut the
// spotlight off at this section's top edge. The page-wide version
// doesn't have a per-section box to clip in the first place, so this
// section needs nothing beyond the `.halftone-hover` class itself.
//
// File size: you've since swapped the original ~45MB base64-in-SVG
// export for other_island_2.webp (~2.4MB) — a big improvement, though
// still worth a look if you want to shave it down further (a well
// compressed webp of a similar sky/landscape scene can often get well
// under 1MB with no visible quality loss). `loading="lazy"` is still
// on the <img> below so it doesn't compete with anything above it for
// bandwidth on initial load either way.
//
// Usage:
//   <OtherIslandSection
//     id="section-closing"
//     image="/images/other_island.svg"
//   />

interface Props {
  id: string;
  image: string;
  alt?: string;
}

const { id, image, alt = "" } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="other-section sky-chain halftone-hover" id={id}>
  <img class="other-section-image" src={image} alt={alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
</section>

<style>
  .other-section {
    position: relative;
    width: 100vw;
    left: 50%;
    right: 50%;
    margin-left: -50vw;
    margin-right: -50vw;
    overflow-x: hidden;
    line-height: 0;
  }

  .other-section-image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    /* height (not min-height) — see the component-level comment above
       for why the distinction matters here. */
    .other-section {
      height: 100vh;
    }

    /* object-fit: cover fills that box by cropping (mostly the sides,
       since the source is much wider than tall); object-position: top
       keeps the crop anchored to the top edge specifically — see the
       comment above for why that edge can't be the one that moves. */
    .other-section-image {
      width: 100%;
      height: 100%;
      object-fit: cover;
      object-position: top;
    }
  }
</style>